[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Transport Security and Threat Mitigation in REST APIs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing REST APIs requires a multi-layered approach that addresses threats at the network transport layer, the browser client layer, and the application processing layer. This tutorial covers critical security controls including TLS configuration, CORS policies, CSRF defense, XSS prevention, and Rate Limiting."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Transport Layer Security (TLS)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TLS encrypts data in transit between the client and the REST API, preventing eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks. APIs should exclusively use TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3 and enforce HTTPS strictly using HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS)."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name api.example.com;

    ssl_certificate /path/to/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /path/to/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;

    add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"CORS is a security mechanism implemented by browsers to restrict web applications from making requests to a different domain than the one that served the web page. Misconfigured CORS headers can allow malicious sites to read sensitive API responses."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"app.use(cors({
    origin: 'https://trusted-client-app.com',
    methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
    allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
    credentials: true,
    maxAge: 600
}));"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSRF occurs when a malicious site tricks a user's browser into executing an unwanted action on a trusted site where the user is currently authenticated. For stateless REST APIs using token-based authentication stored in LocalStorage, CSRF is typically not an issue. However, if using cookie-based authentication, explicit mitigations are required."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the Synchronizer Token Pattern or Double Submit Cookie Pattern."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set the SameSite attribute on cookies to 'Strict' or 'Lax' to prevent browsers from sending cookies on cross-site requests."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"res.cookie('session_token', token, {
    httpOnly: true,
    secure: true,
    sameSite: 'strict'
});"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Prevention"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"XSS vulnerabilities allow attackers to inject malicious scripts into web pages viewed by other users. If an API accepts unvalidated input and reflects it back or stores it, downstream clients may execute malicious scripts, leading to stolen tokens."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always sanitize and validate input on the server side."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Encode output data properly depending on the context (HTML, JSON, Attribute)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Store sensitive JWTs in memory or HttpOnly cookies instead of localStorage to minimize token theft via XSS."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Rate Limiting and Brute-Force Mitigation"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Rate limiting protects REST APIs from Denial of Service (DoS) attacks, credential stuffing, and brute-force attacks by restricting the number of requests a client can make within a specified time window."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const apiLimiter = rateLimit({
    windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
    max: 100,
    standardHeaders: true,
    legacyHeaders: false,
    message: 'Too many requests created from this IP, please try again after 15 minutes'
});

app.use('/api/', apiLimiter);"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"By enforcing strict TLS configurations, configuring precise CORS policies, implementing CSRF tokens or SameSite cookies, sanitizing data against XSS, and applying rate limits, developers can significantly harden their REST APIs against modern web vulnerabilities."}]
